WEBVTT

01:48.946 --> 01:50.448
(SCREAMING)

01:53.200 --> 01:55.870
My jewels! My jewels!

01:55.953 --> 01:59.040
I've been robbed!
Someone stole my jewels!

01:59.123 --> 02:01.917
Help! Help! Police!

02:18.559 --> 02:19.602
(WOMAN SCREAMING)

02:19.727 --> 02:24.607
My diamonds! My diamonds!
They're gone! Help!

02:43.918 --> 02:48.005
(WOMAN SHOUTING IN FRENCH)

02:50.508 --> 02:54.095
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

04:07.042 --> 04:09.420
(TIRES SCREECHING)

04:42.661 --> 04:43.954
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

05:15.152 --> 05:16.570
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

05:17.112 --> 05:18.155
(DOOR OPENING)

05:24.829 --> 05:26.038
(HOUSEKEEPER CALLING
IN FRENCH)

05:31.752 --> 05:33.254
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

05:35.172 --> 05:36.215
(THANKING IN FRENCH)

05:40.719 --> 05:42.429
- Lepic.
- Mercier.

05:43.681 --> 05:46.100
<i>Monsieur</i> Robie,
we represent the Sûreté.

05:46.183 --> 05:47.726
We are making some inquiries

05:47.810 --> 05:50.271
in reference
to a number of jewel robberies.

05:50.354 --> 05:53.607
We have reason to believe that
you could provide us with information

05:53.691 --> 05:56.151
that would help us in our investigations.

05:56.235 --> 06:00.114
Would you be good enough
to accompany us to our office in Nice?

06:09.790 --> 06:13.043
All right, but would you mind
if I put on something more formal?

06:14.169 --> 06:15.212
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

06:26.682 --> 06:27.725
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

06:33.731 --> 06:35.107
(GUN FIRING)

06:56.837 --> 06:57.880
(CAR ENGINE REVVING)

06:57.963 --> 06:59.006
(TIRES SCREECH)

08:38.605 --> 08:41.859
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

09:31.033 --> 09:33.702
(CHIRPING)

10:34.596 --> 10:35.639
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

11:35.365 --> 11:37.701
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

11:38.202 --> 11:39.244
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

11:39.536 --> 11:40.871
Okay.

11:41.580 --> 11:42.998
You left in a hurry, huh?

11:43.290 --> 11:45.792
- They came for me an hour ago.
- The police?

11:45.876 --> 11:49.296
- Five of them.
- Naturally, you are innocent.

11:50.005 --> 11:52.966
I haven't stolen a piece of jewelry
in 15 years.

11:53.050 --> 11:56.428
- Honesty.
- Well, it has a good feeling.

11:56.511 --> 12:00.098
What do you think of my kitchen?
Works like a machine, yes?

12:00.182 --> 12:02.226
- Bertani...
- Just like our little band

12:02.309 --> 12:07.648
in the Underground during the war.
Cutting, slicing, just like the old days.

12:08.148 --> 12:10.484
They think I'm responsible
for these robberies, don't they?

12:10.567 --> 12:13.153
Well, once they were all in prison
with you.

12:13.237 --> 12:15.906
I know. And now if there's
any crime on the Riviera,

12:15.989 --> 12:17.908
we're the first to be suspected
by the police.

12:17.991 --> 12:21.245
But since the war, I have obliged
all these men to be honest.

12:21.328 --> 12:23.497
I have beat honesty into their skulls.

12:23.622 --> 12:26.667
I did not want them to break
their paroles and return to prison.

12:26.750 --> 12:29.753
Listen, if my parole is broken,
they'll throw away the key.

12:29.836 --> 12:32.756
I came here for one reason,
to tell these men and you

12:32.839 --> 12:34.758
that I had nothing to do
with the robberies.

12:34.841 --> 12:37.344
Perhaps I believe you,
but your comrades

12:37.427 --> 12:41.265
are very angry with you, Robie.
They think you let them down.

12:41.348 --> 12:43.517
So long, Bertani.
I wouldn't put it past any of them

12:43.600 --> 12:45.602
to be doing the robberies themselves.

12:45.686 --> 12:49.106
Simple men without education?
No, Robie.

12:49.189 --> 12:50.274
(WOMAN SPEAKING FRENCH)

12:50.357 --> 12:51.942
Excuse me. Business calls.

12:52.526 --> 12:54.027
Don't go.

13:10.961 --> 13:12.004
(GREETING IN FRENCH)

13:20.887 --> 13:21.972
(MAN SPEAKING FRENCH)

13:35.902 --> 13:37.487
(PEOPLE LAUGHING)

13:37.738 --> 13:40.157
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

13:50.375 --> 13:53.420
But, you know,
coincidence can be terrible.

13:53.545 --> 13:57.758
These robberies all bear your mark,
but you claim to be innocent.

13:57.841 --> 14:00.177
I do more than claim. I insist.

14:03.388 --> 14:07.184
What I can't understand is how
this thief could imitate me so perfectly.

14:07.267 --> 14:11.021
It has to be someone
who knew every detail of my technique.

14:11.104 --> 14:13.106
Maybe somebody in the police.

14:13.190 --> 14:14.191
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

14:14.274 --> 14:15.359
(BERTANI CHUCKLES)

14:16.109 --> 14:19.279
He picks perfect victims
with only the right stones.

14:19.363 --> 14:22.115
Goes up walls, over the roofs,
down through the skylights,

14:22.199 --> 14:24.618
leaves no clue
and then disappears in the night.

14:24.701 --> 14:26.787
Just like John Robie, the Cat.

14:26.870 --> 14:28.622
You don't believe me
any more than the rest.

14:28.705 --> 14:30.957
Oh, he's as nervous as a cat.

14:31.291 --> 14:34.628
If somebody caught this imitator,
we'd all be off the hook, wouldn't we?

14:34.753 --> 14:36.463
- Surely.
- Well, no one believes me,

14:36.588 --> 14:38.882
but the police
are chasing the wrong man.

14:38.965 --> 14:41.051
Someone's got to start
chasing the right one.

14:41.134 --> 14:43.136
One day he'll make a mistake.

14:43.261 --> 14:46.306
Bertani, there's only one answer,
and I should have seen it sooner.

14:46.431 --> 14:51.144
- I've got to catch this imitator myself.
- You couldn't do more than the police.

14:51.269 --> 14:54.231
Oh, but I could,
and I'm the only one who could,

14:54.314 --> 14:57.401
because I can anticipate him,
try to figure out his next move,

14:57.484 --> 14:58.985
and then get there ahead of him

14:59.069 --> 15:01.405
and catch him
with his hand right in the jewel case.

15:01.488 --> 15:04.324
If they catch you,
nobody will believe what you say.

15:04.449 --> 15:08.495
Who believes me now?
The biggest problem is time.

15:08.578 --> 15:11.498
I've got to hit this copycat
before he hears I'm after him.

15:11.623 --> 15:14.167
To catch him in the act,
I need better information than he has,

15:14.251 --> 15:15.752
the kind that takes months to dig out.

15:15.836 --> 15:19.673
Like who has jewels
that deserve to be stolen?

15:19.798 --> 15:22.259
Yeah. Where they live,
which room they keep the stones in,

15:22.342 --> 15:24.511
what time they usually go to sleep,
how much they drink,

15:24.636 --> 15:27.180
whether they have dogs,
guns, servants, insurance.

15:27.305 --> 15:30.600
Well, for old times' sakes,
perhaps I can help you.

15:32.602 --> 15:36.189
- Come on, Bertani. What have you got?
- You made me remember something.

15:36.273 --> 15:38.859
Two days ago,
a man came into this restaurant.

15:38.942 --> 15:40.944
- I did not like him.
- Why not?

15:41.027 --> 15:42.446
He called me to his table.

15:42.529 --> 15:45.198
He asked me about crime and criminals,

15:45.323 --> 15:48.535
me, a respectable, honest restaurateur.

15:48.660 --> 15:50.620
You think he may know something
about the robberies?

15:50.704 --> 15:54.541
He asks me strange questions.
I refuse to answer.

15:55.041 --> 15:58.712
Then he asked me
about the jewels my clients wear,

15:58.795 --> 16:03.341
and I said to myself, "You have
something to do with this new Cat.

16:03.717 --> 16:06.052
"Maybe you are the new Cat."

16:06.219 --> 16:10.557
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

16:25.572 --> 16:30.744
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

16:31.161 --> 16:35.624
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

16:35.916 --> 16:38.126
BERTANI: Foussard's daughter
will take you out of here by boat.

16:38.251 --> 16:39.419
- Where?
- The beach club in Cannes.

16:39.544 --> 16:41.463
Wait for a phone call there.

16:57.479 --> 17:00.106
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

17:06.613 --> 17:09.449
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

17:13.328 --> 17:16.289
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

17:17.290 --> 17:18.917
Okay, Mr. Cat, let's go.

17:19.000 --> 17:22.087
Danielle, do me a favor.
Don't call me a cat.

17:22.170 --> 17:24.297
I only do one favor a day.

17:24.464 --> 17:25.465
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

17:25.966 --> 17:27.509
Will you do as your father here asks?

17:27.634 --> 17:30.303
Did I brush your fur the wrong way?

17:34.307 --> 17:36.601
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

17:46.486 --> 17:47.821
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

18:06.548 --> 18:08.174
Hey, you're getting us wet.

18:08.300 --> 18:11.928
It must be true, what they say.
Cats don't like water.

18:12.012 --> 18:14.639
I'll thank you
not to mention that word again.

18:14.723 --> 18:17.559
Oh, a man should never regret his past.

18:17.851 --> 18:22.230
- I only regret one thing.
- That you never asked me to marry you?

18:22.355 --> 18:25.233
No, that I ever took the time
to teach you English.

18:25.358 --> 18:29.321
You only taught me the nouns.
I learned the adjectives myself.

18:29.404 --> 18:32.824
- The word "cat" is a noun.
- Not the way you use it.

18:32.907 --> 18:37.495
For you it means excitement,
danger, affluence.

18:37.579 --> 18:40.206
What do you think
of that word, "affluence"?

18:40.332 --> 18:42.250
It means wealth.

18:43.543 --> 18:45.378
What's on your mind?

18:45.503 --> 18:48.173
Nothing. I was just thinking about you.

18:48.256 --> 18:52.594
Imagining you in your expensive villa,
enjoying life,

18:52.719 --> 18:55.472
while we work like idiots
for a loaf of bread.

18:55.555 --> 18:57.974
I work for a living, too,
raising grapes and flowers.

18:58.058 --> 19:00.894
And rubies and diamonds and pearls.

19:03.229 --> 19:06.566
School's open again.
Professor Robie will now conduct a class

19:06.691 --> 19:09.277
in bad manners,
or how to get spanked in a hurry.

19:09.402 --> 19:12.405
You can't touch me. I've graduated.

19:12.864 --> 19:17.243
- You going to South America?
- No, just the beach club at Cannes.

19:17.535 --> 19:20.872
I've always dreamed
of going to South America.

19:20.955 --> 19:23.583
People say it's a virgin country.

19:24.417 --> 19:28.630
I can cook, sew, keep my mouth shut

19:29.089 --> 19:32.092
and peddle stolen jewels
on the black market.

19:33.968 --> 19:36.429
Now, Danielle,
you don't think I'm responsible

19:36.554 --> 19:38.348
for all those recent robberies?

19:38.431 --> 19:40.809
- I think so, yes.
- Yes.

19:40.934 --> 19:43.895
Together with your father
and the rest of my old Resistance pals.

19:43.978 --> 19:46.314
But there's one great difference.

19:46.439 --> 19:49.025
They are furious with you. I am not.

19:49.109 --> 19:52.404
Danielle, listen carefully.
I stole once a long time ago.

19:52.487 --> 19:54.155
- I went to jail.
- I know.

19:54.280 --> 19:56.449
The Germans bombed the prison
and you all escaped,

19:56.574 --> 19:58.576
joined the Underground
and became heroes.

19:58.660 --> 20:02.038
I joined because I wanted to make up
for some of the things I'd done.

20:02.122 --> 20:04.708
- I've never stolen since.
- I don't know what you wanted.

20:04.791 --> 20:08.044
I know what you got.
Pardons that are not worth anything.

20:08.128 --> 20:10.088
They weren't pardons,
they were paroles,

20:10.171 --> 20:12.632
and we fought for six years
to earn them,

20:12.757 --> 20:15.093
those of us who were still around
for the graduation.

20:15.176 --> 20:17.846
Those paroles don't have
much value today, huh?

20:17.971 --> 20:20.932
Well, at least they haven't been
withdrawn from circulation.

20:21.015 --> 20:22.726
No South America?

20:22.809 --> 20:23.977
(AIRPLANE HUMMING)

20:24.102 --> 20:26.771
No, just the beach club
at Cannes, please.

20:26.855 --> 20:30.024
Well, in that case, we should hurry.

20:30.150 --> 20:33.653
That airplane up there,
it probably belongs to the police.

20:37.115 --> 20:39.826
- Hey, <i>stupide,</i> the contact.
- Oh.

20:49.836 --> 20:51.838
What's he doing now?

20:53.173 --> 20:54.632
He's coming back over us.

20:54.716 --> 20:57.218
Good. When he comes by again,
wave to him.

20:57.343 --> 20:59.971
- Suppose he's not my type?
- Oh, wave to him anyway.

21:00.054 --> 21:03.016
Act as if you're a pretty girl
just out for a ride.

21:03.099 --> 21:04.267
Well...

21:07.896 --> 21:09.022
Whoo-hoo!

21:10.607 --> 21:14.194
Hey, hey, not that pretty.
We want to get rid of him.

21:15.528 --> 21:18.573
- How much further to the beach club?
- Oh, about 15 minutes.

21:20.074 --> 21:22.118
Well, when you get there,
pull close in shore

21:22.202 --> 21:23.703
and mingle around with the other boats.

21:23.787 --> 21:24.829
And then?

21:24.913 --> 21:27.665
And then I'm getting out.
I'll leave my clothes with you.

21:27.749 --> 21:30.710
And I thought
you hoped to be inconspicuous.

21:30.794 --> 21:33.588
Well, nobody will ever recognize me
in these.

22:24.806 --> 22:25.890
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

22:39.988 --> 22:42.907
- Hello?
- The man I speak with you about,

22:42.991 --> 22:45.785
the man who asked me
the questions about the jewels,

22:45.910 --> 22:49.080
he will wait for you at the entrance
of the flower market in Nice.

22:49.163 --> 22:54.335
He will find you. I told him
you would be tossing a coin in the air.

23:42.842 --> 23:44.510
Tails?

23:51.517 --> 23:53.895
H.H. Hughson, Lloyd's of London.

23:54.020 --> 23:56.314
Am I to understand
you're the man who knows everyone

23:56.397 --> 23:59.233
who owns the best jewelry
in this vicinity?

23:59.359 --> 24:01.486
We insure most of the important pieces.

24:01.569 --> 24:04.739
Insurance. That's gambling, isn't it?

24:04.864 --> 24:06.908
Well, shall we say betting?

24:07.033 --> 24:10.411
Yes, let's just say betting,
because I have a long shot for you.

24:10.536 --> 24:13.498
A little help
in return for some of your losses.

24:13.581 --> 24:16.876
- So Mr. Bertani told me.
- Are you interested?

24:17.001 --> 24:21.547
Well, the proposition sounds
intriguing, albeit a little unorthodox.

24:22.548 --> 24:24.425
What does that mean? Yes or no?

24:24.550 --> 24:26.678
- My dear Mr. Robie...
- Smith.

24:26.761 --> 24:29.097
I beg your pardon. Ever been married?

24:29.222 --> 24:31.516
No, but what has that to do
with yes or no?

24:31.599 --> 24:33.935
It might help you
to understand my problem.

24:34.060 --> 24:36.646
I have two wives,
Felicity, God bless her,

24:36.729 --> 24:39.482
and the London office.
I must return worthy of both of them.

24:39.565 --> 24:42.151
I see. And you don't think
they'd approve of your giving me

24:42.235 --> 24:44.195
a list of your richest clients.

24:44.278 --> 24:47.573
Officially, you come under
the category of extremely bad risk.

24:47.657 --> 24:48.658
Uh-huh.

24:52.120 --> 24:53.538
Well, see you later, Hughson.

24:53.621 --> 24:55.832
It's always a pleasure
to meet an insurance agent

24:55.915 --> 24:58.960
who enjoys paying off
such expensive claims.

25:01.921 --> 25:04.549
However, unofficially,
there's hope for you.

25:04.632 --> 25:06.050
I was sure there would be.

25:06.134 --> 25:08.094
We're both taking a big chance,
you know.

25:08.219 --> 25:11.264
Really? What happens to you
if I'm caught?

25:11.389 --> 25:14.559
I might be embarrassed,
even censured officially.

25:14.642 --> 25:18.229
- They could put me away for good.
- You made a bad choice of professions.

25:18.312 --> 25:20.398
Well, then let's come
to an understanding.

25:20.481 --> 25:22.400
I'm doing you a favor.

25:22.483 --> 25:25.028
I take all the risks,
you get all the jewelry back.

25:25.111 --> 25:29.157
Mr. Smith, it strikes me that only
an honest man would be so foolish.

25:29.282 --> 25:30.742
Thank you.

25:33.828 --> 25:36.414
- How much of a list do you need?
- Only the top half-dozen names.

25:36.497 --> 25:38.916
- Anything else?
- Oh, the addresses, habits,

25:39.000 --> 25:42.795
whatever you've got.
Descriptions of the stones and settings.

25:44.297 --> 25:46.758
- Suppose it falls into the wrong hands?
- Perhaps it already has,

25:46.841 --> 25:48.426
unless you've been
pulling the thefts yourself.

25:48.509 --> 25:49.635
I say, that's not a bad idea.

25:49.761 --> 25:51.262
- And where are you staying?
- Carlton, Cannes.

25:51.345 --> 25:52.513
Okay.

25:59.520 --> 26:03.149
(CROWD CLAMORING)

26:17.038 --> 26:18.831
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

26:37.183 --> 26:39.894
(BOTH SHOUTING IN FRENCH)

26:41.229 --> 26:43.189
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

27:06.838 --> 27:10.049
No, no, no, my dear fellow,
not in the middle of the day.

27:10.716 --> 27:11.717
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

27:13.052 --> 27:14.095
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

27:14.679 --> 27:16.681
Bring it to the table with you.

27:22.228 --> 27:23.604
Come along.

27:32.196 --> 27:36.242
Under the present circumstances,
do you think it pays to advertise?

27:36.409 --> 27:38.744
He hasn't left the villa in years.

27:41.372 --> 27:44.000
- Well, how do you like the place?
- Immensely.

27:44.083 --> 27:46.210
It's a kind of travel-folder heaven,

27:46.294 --> 27:49.380
where a man dreams he'll go
when he retires.

27:50.965 --> 27:54.886
Now, about that list of your clients
who have jewelry worth stealing.

27:55.052 --> 27:56.888
Look, why don't we enjoy our lunch first?

27:56.971 --> 27:58.556
There's plenty of time.

27:58.639 --> 28:00.641
Mr. Hughson,
I don't want to seem impatient,

28:00.766 --> 28:03.519
but in 10 days, I have to come up
with something pretty convincing

28:03.603 --> 28:05.771
for the examining magistrate.

28:05.897 --> 28:08.566
Now, it's a very nice custom
they have here in France,

28:08.649 --> 28:11.110
provisional liberty
based upon insufficient evidence.

28:11.235 --> 28:13.946
But, alas, that may not last long for me.

28:14.280 --> 28:17.742
Bertani said you were something of
a celebrity in the Underground Army.

28:17.950 --> 28:19.744
I was in the Resistance.

28:19.827 --> 28:22.788
- Did you kill many people?
- Seventy-two.

28:25.291 --> 28:27.293
I know what would have pleased you,
though, Hughson.

28:27.418 --> 28:29.629
Not one of them was insured.

28:30.671 --> 28:33.966
You're a man of obvious good taste in...
Well, in everything.

28:34.091 --> 28:36.177
How did you... I mean, why did you...

28:36.302 --> 28:38.304
- You mean, why did I take up stealing?
- Yes.

28:38.429 --> 28:40.890
Oh, to live better,
to own things I couldn't afford.

28:40.973 --> 28:43.392
To acquire this good taste
which you now enjoy,

28:43.476 --> 28:45.853
and which I should be
very reluctant to give up.

28:45.978 --> 28:47.772
Oh, you mean
you were frankly dishonest?

28:47.855 --> 28:49.440
I tried to be.

28:49.815 --> 28:53.069
You know, I thought you'd have
some defense, some tale of hardship.

28:53.152 --> 28:54.820
Your mother ran off
when you were young,

28:54.946 --> 28:57.156
your father beat you or something.

28:57.240 --> 28:59.742
No, no, no, I was a member
of an American trapeze act

28:59.825 --> 29:01.786
in a circus that traveled in Europe.

29:02.328 --> 29:03.663
It folded and I was stranded,

29:03.788 --> 29:06.499
so I put my agility
to a more rewarding purpose.

29:06.624 --> 29:08.334
You have no other defense?

29:08.459 --> 29:11.504
Well, for what it's worth, I only stole
from people who wouldn't go hungry.

29:11.629 --> 29:12.838
Your plate.

29:14.340 --> 29:16.592
This is a quiche Lorraine.
I think you'll enjoy this.

29:16.676 --> 29:18.761
Quiche... Yes, I've heard of it.
I've never had it, though.

29:18.844 --> 29:21.430
- It looks delicious.
- Yes.

29:23.849 --> 29:27.436
Oh, it's wonderful.
And the pastry is as light as air.

29:27.520 --> 29:29.605
Well, Germaine
has very sensitive hands,

29:29.689 --> 29:32.692
-an exceedingly light touch.
- Yes, I can tell.

29:32.775 --> 29:36.195
She strangled a German general once

29:36.654 --> 29:38.322
without a sound.

29:42.451 --> 29:45.705
What an extraordinary woman. I...

29:46.706 --> 29:49.292
I take it
you were a sort of modern Robin Hood.

29:49.375 --> 29:52.837
I mean, you gave away
most of the proceeds of your crimes.

29:53.212 --> 29:54.880
Kept everything myself.

29:56.632 --> 30:00.011
Well, let's face it.
I was an out-and-out thief, like you.

30:00.303 --> 30:03.055
- Oh, I say, steady, old man.
- Wait a minute, wait a minute.

30:03.139 --> 30:05.808
Did you ever take an ashtray
from a hotel, or a towel?

30:05.891 --> 30:07.893
Souvenirs. They expect that.

30:08.561 --> 30:11.063
You're given an expense allowance
to pay for all the meals

30:11.147 --> 30:14.567
you eat on the job, right?
But this meal is free.

30:14.692 --> 30:17.153
Now, are you going to deduct
the price of a lunch

30:17.236 --> 30:19.488
from your expense account?

30:19.572 --> 30:22.241
But of course you're not.
It would be stupid. Do you agree?

30:22.325 --> 30:24.660
- Yes.
- You're a thief.

30:24.744 --> 30:26.412
Well, only an amateur thief, of course,

30:26.495 --> 30:29.248
but it'll help you to sympathize
with us professionals.

30:29.332 --> 30:32.835
- I don't think I understand fully.
- Well, now, let's look at it this way.

30:32.918 --> 30:35.921
You're sorry you took the ashtray
or the towel from the hotel, aren't you?

30:36.005 --> 30:38.424
- Well, right now, yes.
- And someday you're going to be sorry

30:38.507 --> 30:40.760
that you didn't deduct this lunch
from your expense account.

30:40.843 --> 30:42.261
My dear fellow, I can't possibly deduct

30:42.345 --> 30:43.763
every little item
from my expense account.

30:43.888 --> 30:47.600
- I frankly couldn't spare the time.
- Well, someday you'll wish you had.

30:47.933 --> 30:49.769
Every time an ashtray
is missing from a hotel,

30:49.894 --> 30:51.270
they don't come looking for you.

30:51.395 --> 30:53.439
But let a diamond bracelet
disappear in France,

30:53.522 --> 30:56.108
and they shout, "John Robie, the Cat!"

30:56.609 --> 30:58.611
You don't have to spend
every day of your life

30:58.736 --> 31:00.780
proving your honesty, but I do.

31:01.697 --> 31:04.408
Well, let's get down to business.
The list.

31:09.038 --> 31:10.915
Is something bothering you?

31:11.123 --> 31:14.043
I told the police
what you were going to do.

31:15.044 --> 31:18.047
Well, I didn't expect them
to like my arrangement.

31:18.130 --> 31:20.383
You were wrong.
They thought it a splendid idea,

31:20.466 --> 31:21.967
because they hope
you'll make a mistake

31:22.051 --> 31:24.637
and provide them with the evidence
they need against you.

31:24.720 --> 31:27.556
Yes. It had to be something like that.

31:31.143 --> 31:32.978
Suppose the whole thing goes wrong?

31:33.062 --> 31:35.106
Come on, Hughson, you're wasting time.

31:49.412 --> 31:52.998
Quite a thorough job.
Have some wine, Hughson.

31:56.502 --> 31:58.295
Yes, I think I will.

31:59.171 --> 32:01.841
Oh, my. I wish I'd known
someone in the insurance racket

32:01.966 --> 32:04.969
when I first started
in the burglary business.

32:06.512 --> 32:10.433
Well, let me see.
My first bait will be this Mrs. Stevens,

32:10.516 --> 32:13.436
the American woman with the diamonds
and the daughter.

32:13.519 --> 32:16.355
- Do you know them?
- I'm having dinner with them tomorrow.

32:16.439 --> 32:18.607
I could possibly arrange a meeting.

32:19.191 --> 32:22.778
Hughson, in this business,
you can't do things the honest way.

32:22.862 --> 32:24.363
Remember that.

32:34.331 --> 32:37.042
No, no, what I mean, Mrs. Stevens,
wouldn't it perhaps be better

32:37.168 --> 32:39.962
if you left some of that jewelry
in the hotel safe?

32:40.045 --> 32:41.464
Stop acting like an insurance agent.

32:41.547 --> 32:45.176
I didn't buy these things for my old age.
I bought them to wear.

32:46.469 --> 32:48.053
Put your money away, Hughson.

32:48.137 --> 32:50.723
You can cheat a little
on your expense account.

32:55.728 --> 32:58.647
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

32:58.898 --> 33:00.316
That's France.

33:00.483 --> 33:02.735
Yes, and everyone from
the vegetable scrubber to the manager

33:02.860 --> 33:05.738
gets a tip, whether he's earned it or not.
It's the law.

33:05.821 --> 33:08.657
Mother, everywhere you go,
you complain about tipping.

33:08.741 --> 33:12.077
And I shall continue to complain.
I've just paid for the privilege.

33:13.996 --> 33:17.666
Come on. Let's go over to the casino.
I want to hit the tables while they're hot.

33:17.750 --> 33:20.336
Perhaps I should
just mail them the money.

33:20.419 --> 33:23.589
Oh, no. No, don't bother, Pierre.
I can't afford it.

33:27.885 --> 33:31.096
Handsome. I wouldn't mind buying
that for you.

33:31.222 --> 33:33.015
- Oh, Mother.
- Come along.

33:33.098 --> 33:34.934
Maybe Mr. Hughson
doesn't care for gambling.

33:35.017 --> 33:38.020
Everyone likes to gamble,
one way or another, even you.

33:38.103 --> 33:39.939
I have an intense dislike for it.

33:40.022 --> 33:43.442
Francie, dear,
when the stakes are right, you'll gamble.

33:44.109 --> 33:46.570
ROBIE: It's an imitation? Really?

33:47.446 --> 33:50.449
You mean costume jewelry?
Well, what do you know?

33:50.533 --> 33:52.284
The things they make these days.

33:52.368 --> 33:54.370
Why, you can hardly tell it
from the real thing.

33:54.453 --> 33:57.289
Well, it's better than anything
we have back in Portland, Oregon.

33:57.414 --> 33:59.458
Almost everything is.

33:59.917 --> 34:00.960
Thank you.

34:11.303 --> 34:14.306
I should never have stopped here.
Baccarat's my game.

34:14.390 --> 34:17.726
Why did you let me get so close
to this whirling pickpocket, anyway?

34:17.977 --> 34:20.896
(MAN SPEAKING FRENCH)

34:20.980 --> 34:22.648
Wouldn't you know?

34:50.509 --> 34:51.594
(WOMAN GASPS)

34:52.177 --> 34:53.762
Oh, I'm terribly sorry, madam. I'm...

34:53.846 --> 34:55.598
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

35:01.520 --> 35:03.647
Yes, but, madam, that...

35:05.024 --> 35:07.818
Well, that was a 10,000 franc plaque.

35:09.778 --> 35:13.198
(TRANSLATING IN FRENCH)

35:17.369 --> 35:19.830
Well, maybe she doesn't...

35:20.372 --> 35:24.376
Well, of course, madam, if you'd
rather not take my word, it's all right.

35:27.546 --> 35:31.216
Well, thank you. I'll trust you, too.
I won't count it.

35:32.217 --> 35:34.345
(LAUGHING)

35:43.896 --> 35:46.023
If Jeremiah were only here.

35:46.398 --> 35:49.234
But he'd think all this flying around
from Palm Springs

35:49.318 --> 35:52.571
to Saint Moritz to the Riviera...
He'd think it was all foolishness.

35:52.696 --> 35:56.241
He used to say, "I wouldn't be
one of those silly society gadabouts

35:56.325 --> 35:59.078
"if they promised me I could live forever."

35:59.578 --> 36:01.413
Well, he got his wish.

36:02.581 --> 36:04.750
Mr. Burns,
where'd you say you were from?

36:04.833 --> 36:07.920
- Oregon.
- Jeremiah would have liked you.

36:08.420 --> 36:11.924
A man with both feet on the ground,
that's what he was.

36:12.007 --> 36:14.760
Unfortunately for him,
he never realized how valuable

36:14.843 --> 36:16.929
the ground was he had his feet on.

36:17.012 --> 36:19.264
- We had a ranch, you see. It wasn't...
- Mother, please.

36:19.348 --> 36:21.600
Oh, Mr. Burns would be very interested.

36:21.725 --> 36:24.103
We had a ranch.
It wasn't a very big one.

36:24.853 --> 36:27.523
No plumbing. Little thing out back.

36:28.774 --> 36:29.775
Poor Jeremiah.

36:29.900 --> 36:33.278
He'll never know how close he came
to 20 million barrels of oil.

36:37.783 --> 36:39.368
Bourbon's the only drink.

36:39.451 --> 36:42.454
You can take all that champagne stuff
and pour it down the English Channel.

36:42.579 --> 36:45.791
Well, why wait 80 years
before you can drink the stuff?

36:45.958 --> 36:49.712
Grape vineyards,
huge barrels aging forever,

36:50.421 --> 36:52.464
poor little old monks
running around testing it,

36:52.548 --> 36:56.385
just so some woman in Tulsa,
Oklahoma, can say it tickles her nose.

36:56.802 --> 36:59.471
Mother, I think we ought to go to bed.

37:01.140 --> 37:03.642
Nobody ever calls me Jessie anymore.

37:06.812 --> 37:09.148
Mr. Burns, would you call me Jessie?

37:09.231 --> 37:11.150
- I'd be happy to.
- Good.

37:14.153 --> 37:16.321
Mr. Hughson, would you call me Jessie?

37:16.405 --> 37:18.490
- If you like.
- I like.

37:18.574 --> 37:22.661
- Mr. Burns, you said lumber?
- That's right.

37:22.745 --> 37:25.164
How come you haven't made a pass
at my daughter?

37:25.289 --> 37:27.666
And don't say "oh, Mother" to me.

37:29.334 --> 37:31.795
Mr. Burns, I asked you a question.

37:32.171 --> 37:34.590
Very pretty. Quietly attractive.

37:35.966 --> 37:37.676
Yeah, but too nice.

37:37.760 --> 37:39.595
Sorry I ever sent her
to that finishing school.

37:39.678 --> 37:41.513
I think they finished her there.

37:41.680 --> 37:44.600
- Come on, Mother.
- And so to bed,

37:45.267 --> 37:47.770
where I can cuddle up to my jewelry.

37:47.853 --> 37:51.523
You know, Mr. Hughson,
as rare and wonderful as they are,

37:51.648 --> 37:54.526
I think I'd rather have
100,000 Jeremiahs.

37:54.610 --> 37:56.445
Well, I think I'll toddle along to my cot.

37:56.528 --> 37:58.697
I'll be delighted
to escort you to your suite.

37:58.822 --> 38:01.533
That's very thoughtful of you, Mr. Burns.
Come on, Jessie.

38:01.617 --> 38:04.870
Mr. Burns,
do you make much money at lumber?

38:04.953 --> 38:07.706
Well, right now building is booming.

38:08.665 --> 38:11.043
Would you mind
if I had you investigated a little?

38:11.168 --> 38:13.504
Not at all. With what object?

38:14.046 --> 38:17.508
Well, if I were Francie's age,
you'd sound too good to be true.

38:31.897 --> 38:33.732
Thank you, Mr. Burns. That...

38:34.817 --> 38:37.736
You know,
there's very little lumber around here.

38:37.861 --> 38:40.155
Just why did you come
to the Riviera, anyway?

38:40.239 --> 38:42.658
To meet someone as charming as you.

38:43.242 --> 38:46.245
Boy, now I am
going to have you investigated.

38:47.246 --> 38:50.332
- Aren't you going in?
- I'm down the other end.

40:09.995 --> 40:12.497
How much did he get away with
last night?

40:12.581 --> 40:16.001
The gems were insured
for 35,000 in dollars.

40:16.335 --> 40:18.086
Somebody wins, somebody loses.

40:18.170 --> 40:20.339
Hughson, I sympathize with you.

40:20.464 --> 40:23.175
Pretty rough, having to send
bad news like that into your home office.

40:23.258 --> 40:25.177
I insured <i>Madame</i> Leroux personally.

40:25.594 --> 40:29.348
Yeah. Well, at least you know that the...
You know, the burglar.

40:29.473 --> 40:31.767
- What do they call him? The...
- The Cat.

40:31.850 --> 40:33.268
Oh, yes.

40:34.353 --> 40:38.106
Well, at least you know he's still around
and getting closer all the time.

40:38.190 --> 40:39.524
That's something, isn't it?

40:39.608 --> 40:43.862
Mrs. Stevens, would you kindly
keep your jewelry in the hotel safe?

40:43.987 --> 40:46.365
And what do I do when I go out,
wear the safe around my neck?

40:46.490 --> 40:49.952
- Well, no, not literally. It's just that...
- It's just that your insurance company

40:50.035 --> 40:52.204
goes into shock
every time something's stolen.

40:52.287 --> 40:54.706
If you haven't any guts,
why, you shouldn't have taken my bet.

40:54.831 --> 40:58.210
Well, that's what it was, a bet.
Now, you want to welsh?

40:58.543 --> 41:01.129
If your jewels are stolen,
you will be paid, of course,

41:01.213 --> 41:03.382
but we couldn't replace
the sentiment and affection

41:03.465 --> 41:04.967
you have for those particular pieces.

41:05.050 --> 41:08.136
Mr. Hughson, I have no more affection
for that jewelry

41:08.220 --> 41:11.181
than I have for a train ticket
that gets me somewhere.

41:11.306 --> 41:13.558
They're pretty and they attract attention.

41:13.725 --> 41:15.560
Most of all,
they make it possible for my daughter

41:15.686 --> 41:18.480
to go to the right places
and not be ashamed of me.

41:18.730 --> 41:20.691
That is, too ashamed of me.

41:20.899 --> 41:23.402
- Good morning, Mr. Hughson.
- Good morning, Miss Stevens.

41:24.820 --> 41:26.822
- Mr. Burns.
- You sent for me?

41:26.905 --> 41:31.326
Yes, I thought we might go for a swim,
or, if you're not athletic, sunbathing.

41:31.410 --> 41:34.413
I think I can manage to stay afloat,
thank you.

41:35.080 --> 41:37.249
Mr. Hughson's been telling us
about a robbery last night

41:37.332 --> 41:39.584
-after we went to bed.
- Oh? Who?

41:39.710 --> 41:42.087
<i>Madame</i> Leroux,
wife of a high government official.

41:42.170 --> 41:45.340
-$35,000.
- Oh, too bad.

41:45.882 --> 41:48.176
You should find a more happy business.

41:48.260 --> 41:51.346
The famous jewel thief, the Cat,
is loose again, they say.

41:51.430 --> 41:55.225
- Well, Mother, you're next.
- I'm insured.

41:55.767 --> 41:58.437
Well, I'll get my bathing trunks.
Meet you in the lobby.

41:58.520 --> 42:00.772
I'll just slip into something
and be down in a few minutes.

42:00.856 --> 42:02.607
Goodbye, Mrs. Stevens.
Good hunting, Hughson.

42:02.733 --> 42:06.528
Just a minute, Mr. Burns.
Weren't we... Weren't we going to...

42:07.112 --> 42:08.280
Weren't we what? Going to what?

42:08.405 --> 42:10.532
Well, last night we discussed going up.

42:10.615 --> 42:12.200
- Up? Up?
- Up. Up.

42:12.284 --> 42:17.289
- Up the funicular railway.
- I can't even spell funicular.

42:19.416 --> 42:21.126
What are you doing this afternoon?

42:21.251 --> 42:23.128
Oh, I went to a real estate agent.

42:23.211 --> 42:25.630
He gave me a list
of furnished villas for rent.

42:25.714 --> 42:30.635
- Oh, do you plan that long a vacation?
- I might even retire here.

42:30.802 --> 42:33.305
It says that some of the villas
aren't in very good repair.

42:33.388 --> 42:36.558
Quite a few of the roofs
need careful examination.

42:37.476 --> 42:40.312
Goodbye, Hughson.
Don't let the robbery spoil your day.

42:40.395 --> 42:42.981
It's only money,
and not even yours at that.

43:00.165 --> 43:01.291
Uh...

43:02.417 --> 43:05.003
Should I ask the social director
to introduce us?

43:05.128 --> 43:08.840
No, no, no. I was just wondering
which was the best way out of here.

43:08.924 --> 43:11.343
Well, the Mediterranean
used to be this way.

43:11.426 --> 43:13.970
Well, I'm a gambler. Let's try that.

43:28.777 --> 43:30.529
There's a message for you.

43:36.034 --> 43:37.369
Thank you.

44:57.782 --> 45:01.244
You performed a very beautiful robbery
last night.

45:01.328 --> 45:04.289
- Strictly routine.
- You're marvelous.

45:04.539 --> 45:06.333
Last night you steal a small fortune,

45:06.416 --> 45:09.419
and today you lie on the beach
with an American beauty.

45:09.502 --> 45:11.838
Well, that's why one needs
a small fortune.

45:11.922 --> 45:13.965
Is this your next victim?

45:14.591 --> 45:16.760
Well, let's just say she's a useful friend.

45:17.886 --> 45:21.097
You know,
your old friends of the Resistance

45:21.181 --> 45:26.144
who work at the restaurant, they called
the police all sorts of terrible names

45:26.228 --> 45:28.480
when they had to let you go yesterday.

45:28.563 --> 45:31.149
Would it be bad manners to ask
who tipped off the police

45:31.233 --> 45:32.484
at the flower market?

45:32.567 --> 45:34.444
Oh, they never say anything to the <i>flics.</i>

45:34.527 --> 45:36.696
- You know that.
- Well, somebody did.

45:36.780 --> 45:41.326
But still, they would be very happy
if you were caught during your next job.

45:41.451 --> 45:43.578
Well, it's nice to know I have friends.

45:43.662 --> 45:46.248
Perhaps it would be better
if you were caught.

45:46.331 --> 45:48.250
Any particular reason?

45:48.333 --> 45:50.210
I heard some talk in the kitchen.

45:50.293 --> 45:53.838
They say, "What a pity
if they must kill the Cat."

45:54.005 --> 45:56.299
They will do all they can
to avoid the prison.

45:56.383 --> 45:59.010
Well, now, isn't that amazing?
The police want me in jail.

45:59.094 --> 46:02.013
My old friends want me dead.
The Cat wants me out of town.

46:02.097 --> 46:04.391
What do you mean,
the Cat wants you out of town?

46:04.474 --> 46:05.934
He sent me a message this morning.

46:06.017 --> 46:08.270
It seems the sky
is about to fall in on me.

46:08.353 --> 46:10.897
Then don't you think
it's foolish to remain here

46:10.981 --> 46:13.525
without knowing
what will happen to you?

46:13.608 --> 46:16.820
But if you were
in South America with me,

46:16.903 --> 46:19.114
you would know
exactly what will happen.

46:19.197 --> 46:21.992
You make it sound dangerous
either way.

46:22.075 --> 46:25.495
It would be so much nicer
to be killed by love, no?

46:25.996 --> 46:28.373
Pardon me
while I get the water out of my ear.

46:28.456 --> 46:32.460
John, you know what sort of men
they are at Bertani's.

46:32.544 --> 46:35.088
Another robbery
and they will do something to you.

46:35.171 --> 46:37.757
Yes, yes. Well, I better get back.

46:39.301 --> 46:42.929
But what has she got more than me,
except money?

46:43.346 --> 46:45.890
And you are getting plenty of that.

46:45.974 --> 46:50.228
Danielle, you are just a girl.
She is a woman.

46:50.937 --> 46:55.775
Why do you want to buy an old car
if you can get a new one cheaper?

46:55.942 --> 46:58.862
It will run better and last longer.

47:01.072 --> 47:03.616
It looks as if my old car just drove off.

47:03.700 --> 47:05.952
No, it hasn't. It's just turned amphibious.

47:06.077 --> 47:07.120
Oh...

47:07.203 --> 47:09.581
I thought I'd come out
and see what the big attraction was.

47:09.664 --> 47:12.417
- Yes.
- And possibly even rate an introduction.

47:12.500 --> 47:14.753
Oh, you didn't tell me your name.

47:14.836 --> 47:17.964
- Danielle Foussard.
- Miss Foussard, Miss Stevens.

47:18.048 --> 47:20.216
How do you do, Miss Foussard?

47:20.300 --> 47:22.802
Mr. Burns has told me
so little about you.

47:23.678 --> 47:25.430
Well, we only met
a couple of minutes ago.

47:25.513 --> 47:27.265
That's right. Only a few minutes ago.

47:27.349 --> 47:31.478
Only a few minutes ago,
and you talk like old friends.

47:31.895 --> 47:34.731
Well, that's warm, friendly France
for you.

47:35.357 --> 47:37.817
Well, I was asking
about renting some water skis.

47:37.901 --> 47:39.944
Would you like me
to teach you how to water ski?

47:40.028 --> 47:41.696
Thank you, but I was women's champion

47:41.780 --> 47:44.032
at Sarasota, Florida, last season.

47:44.115 --> 47:45.992
Well, it was just an idea.

47:46.993 --> 47:49.537
Are you sure
you were talking about water skis?

47:49.621 --> 47:50.663
From where I sat,

47:50.747 --> 47:53.792
it looked as though you were
conjugating some irregular verbs.

47:55.126 --> 47:57.629
Say something nice to her, Danielle.

47:58.338 --> 48:00.590
She looks a lot older up close.

48:03.593 --> 48:07.639
To a mere child,
anything over 20 might seem old.

48:07.931 --> 48:12.894
A child? Shall we stand
in shallower water and discuss that?

48:16.481 --> 48:18.400
Enjoying yourself, Mr. Burns?

48:18.483 --> 48:20.944
Oh, it's very nice out here.
You know, the sun and all.

48:21.027 --> 48:23.321
Well, it's too much for me.
I'll see you at the hotel.

48:23.405 --> 48:26.324
- I'll come with you.
- But, Mr. Burns, you didn't finish

48:26.408 --> 48:29.994
telling me why French women are
more seductive than American women.

48:30.078 --> 48:32.414
You know what I'd like to tell you,
don't you?

48:32.497 --> 48:34.374
Wait a minute!

48:35.166 --> 48:36.209
Oh, for...

49:19.919 --> 49:21.045
Here.

49:22.839 --> 49:23.840
<i>Merci.</i>

49:58.917 --> 50:00.585
Do you have time for me now?

50:00.668 --> 50:02.837
Well, I'm sorry I was so long
out there at the float.

50:02.921 --> 50:05.673
From what I saw of that girl,
I thought you'd be a lot longer.

50:05.757 --> 50:09.719
Yeah. Well, what about cocktails?
6:00 suit you?

50:09.802 --> 50:12.263
Well, we can talk about that on the way.

50:12.347 --> 50:15.016
- To where?
- To rent you a villa.

50:15.934 --> 50:19.312
Miss Stevens, picking out a villa is...
You know, it's a personal thing.

50:19.395 --> 50:22.941
I have my car, and a basket lunch
with chicken and beer.

50:23.024 --> 50:24.567
No, no, no.
It's too much to expect of you.

50:24.651 --> 50:26.778
It'll be a tiring, dusty trip
over mountain roads...

50:26.861 --> 50:28.279
Where you're bound to get lost.

50:28.363 --> 50:30.949
A perfect stranger who doesn't know
a word of the language?

50:31.032 --> 50:33.243
Well, I was going to hire
an English-speaking chauffeur.

50:33.326 --> 50:34.744
You have one.

50:34.869 --> 50:37.956
I'll give you a wholesale rate,
and no tipping.

50:39.249 --> 50:41.834
I must say your terms are generous.

50:44.337 --> 50:47.757
- Too generous to refuse.
- My terms usually are.

50:48.049 --> 50:50.552
- Where's your car?
- It's right there.

50:50.635 --> 50:54.556
Well, I guess I can't seem to
get out of this gracefully, so let's go.

51:46.024 --> 51:47.650
I've been waiting all day
for you to mention

51:47.734 --> 51:50.236
that kiss I gave you last night.

51:51.195 --> 51:54.782
You know, back home in Oregon,
we'd call you a headstrong girl.

51:54.866 --> 51:57.243
Where in Oregon, the Rogue River?

51:58.119 --> 51:59.245
Where were you born?

51:59.329 --> 52:02.874
In a taxi,
halfway between home and the hospital.

52:02.957 --> 52:05.126
I've lived in 27 different towns and cities.

52:05.209 --> 52:06.961
Why? Somebody chasing you?

52:07.045 --> 52:09.922
- Boys.
- Well, you can stop running now.

52:10.256 --> 52:13.426
When I was 10, my father died
and they discovered oil on our land,

52:13.509 --> 52:15.386
and that's when I really started to travel.

52:15.470 --> 52:17.263
You mean the boys' fathers
were chasing you?

52:17.347 --> 52:18.473
Yes.

52:18.556 --> 52:20.308
But I had the funny feeling
that all they wanted

52:20.391 --> 52:22.393
was to get their hands on my money.

52:22.477 --> 52:24.020
Oh, I'm impressed.

52:24.103 --> 52:25.980
Well, on second thought,
back home in Oregon,

52:26.064 --> 52:27.899
we'd have called you
a rich, headstrong girl.

52:27.982 --> 52:31.486
- That would have made it all right.
- Money handles most people.

52:31.569 --> 52:34.238
- Do you honestly believe that?
- I've proved it.

52:34.322 --> 52:37.200
- You're a singular girl.
- Is that good or bad?

52:37.283 --> 52:40.078
Oh, it's good. It's quite good.
You know what you want,

52:40.161 --> 52:42.997
you go out after it,
and nothing stops you from getting it.

52:43.081 --> 52:44.791
You make it sound corny.

52:44.916 --> 52:48.336
Oh, no, you're a jackpot
of admirable character traits.

52:48.419 --> 52:50.046
I already knew that.

52:50.129 --> 52:52.423
Yes, I will say
you do things with dispatch.

52:52.507 --> 52:53.633
No wasted preliminaries.

52:53.716 --> 52:58.638
Not only did I enjoy that kiss last night,
I was awed by the efficiency behind it.

52:58.721 --> 53:01.516
Well, I'm a great believer
in getting down to essentials.

53:01.599 --> 53:05.436
Yes, inviting me for breakfast,
planning a swim, and now this drive.

53:05.520 --> 53:08.606
Miss Stevens,
I must say you are a girl in a million.

53:08.690 --> 53:11.234
That's a routine compliment,
but I'll accept it.

53:12.527 --> 53:16.781
- May I ask you a personal question?
- I've been hoping you would.

53:16.864 --> 53:20.118
What do you expect to get
out of being so nice to me?

53:20.201 --> 53:22.495
Probably a lot more
than you're willing to offer.

53:22.578 --> 53:24.038
I know.

53:24.664 --> 53:27.083
You're here in Europe
to buy a husband, huh?

53:27.166 --> 53:31.087
- The man I want doesn't have a price.
- Well, that eliminates me.

53:54.485 --> 53:57.780
You're absolutely right. Give me
a woman who knows her own mind.

53:57.864 --> 54:01.325
No one gives you a woman like that.
You have to capture her.

54:01.409 --> 54:03.536
- Any particular method?
- Yes.

54:03.619 --> 54:06.789
But it's no good
unless you discover it yourself.

54:08.332 --> 54:10.793
Are you sure this is the right address?

54:10.877 --> 54:13.671
Well, it is a little large,
but it's on the real estate agent's list.

54:13.755 --> 54:16.549
- Let me see.
- Let's look at the gardens first.

54:16.632 --> 54:18.885
No need to bother the people.

54:19.969 --> 54:23.181
- Why don't you own a place like this?
- Palaces are for royalty.

54:23.264 --> 54:26.601
We're just common people
with a bank account.

54:26.684 --> 54:28.853
That sounds
more like your mother than you.

54:28.936 --> 54:30.646
There's not much difference
between us.

54:30.730 --> 54:32.732
A few years and some grammar.

54:32.982 --> 54:35.193
And jewelry. You never wear any.

54:35.318 --> 54:37.945
I don't like cold things touching my skin.

54:38.029 --> 54:40.156
Why don't you invent
some hot diamonds?

54:40.239 --> 54:42.700
I'd rather spend my money
on more tangible excitement.

54:42.784 --> 54:45.870
Tell me,
what do you get a thrill out of most?

54:46.412 --> 54:49.081
I'm still looking for that one.

54:53.711 --> 54:56.881
I thought we were going
to look at the gardens.

55:01.260 --> 55:02.845
I was interested in the architecture.

55:02.929 --> 55:06.224
Mediterranean, turn of the century,
isn't it?

55:11.896 --> 55:15.900
- You never mention your wife.
- Never found the time to get married.

55:16.025 --> 55:18.319
You don't seem to be
pushed for time now.

55:18.402 --> 55:21.322
Or did you just come over
to add interesting items to your diary?

55:21.405 --> 55:25.243
Like the name and description of
that French girl you swam out to meet.

55:25.326 --> 55:27.411
You are husband-hunting, after all.

55:27.495 --> 55:29.664
That wasn't jealousy you heard working,

55:29.747 --> 55:33.000
merely disappointment
in your limited imagination.

55:33.084 --> 55:35.086
Teenage French girls yet.

55:35.378 --> 55:37.296
Oh, I'll bet you snowed her under.

55:37.421 --> 55:40.508
The big, handsome lumberman
from America.

55:40.591 --> 55:42.969
I'll bet you told her
all your trees were sequoias.

55:43.052 --> 55:45.930
Yeah, well, that certainly sounds
like jealousy to me.

55:46.013 --> 55:48.099
Don't be ashamed of it. Let it out.

55:48.349 --> 55:52.436
- You're somewhat egotistical.
- Fighting fire with fire.

55:52.603 --> 55:55.857
- Miss Stevens?
- Yes, Mr. Burns?

55:55.940 --> 55:57.984
- You know what I think?
- About what?

55:58.067 --> 56:00.987
- You.
- I don't really care.

56:03.281 --> 56:04.490
Tell me.

56:05.449 --> 56:09.495
You're an insecure, pampered woman
accustomed to attracting men,

56:10.079 --> 56:11.289
but you're not quite sure

56:11.372 --> 56:14.083
whether they're attracted to you
or to your money.

56:14.917 --> 56:17.962
- You may never know.
- Anything else?

56:18.045 --> 56:20.298
What you need is something
I have neither the time

56:20.381 --> 56:22.300
-nor the inclination to give you.
- Oh?

56:22.383 --> 56:23.885
And just what is that?

56:23.968 --> 56:27.305
Two weeks with a good man
at Niagara Falls.

57:05.885 --> 57:08.679
I'm hungry.
What about opening that picnic basket?

57:08.763 --> 57:11.682
Not till we get to the picnic grounds.

57:11.807 --> 57:15.853
- Which you've already picked out.
- Which I've already picked out.

57:15.937 --> 57:18.314
- Is it far?
- Oh, a few miles.

57:18.397 --> 57:21.484
- Lonely and secluded?
- Naturally.

57:30.284 --> 57:33.412
Well, then why are we
dawdling like this?

57:33.496 --> 57:36.540
That's exactly
what was running through my mind.

57:56.602 --> 57:58.688
(HONKING)

58:59.874 --> 59:03.002
(TIRES SCREECHING)

59:14.722 --> 59:15.765
(CAR HORN HONKING)

59:49.048 --> 59:50.091
(CAR HORN HONKING)

59:52.968 --> 59:54.011
(CAR CRASHING)

01:00:00.893 --> 01:00:03.479
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:00:09.985 --> 01:00:11.529
Hey, slow down.

01:00:11.612 --> 01:00:14.740
- And let them catch us?
- Let who catch us?

01:00:14.824 --> 01:00:17.493
The police in the black car.
The ones who are following you.

01:00:17.576 --> 01:00:20.121
I don't know what you're talking about.
Police following me?

01:00:20.204 --> 01:00:23.833
Yes, police following you,
John Robie, the Cat.

01:00:48.190 --> 01:00:49.608
Lovely day.

01:00:49.692 --> 01:00:52.570
Have you ever seen
any place in the world more beautiful?

01:00:52.653 --> 01:00:55.447
Just look at the colors
of the sea down there and the sky,

01:00:55.531 --> 01:00:59.285
and those little
pink and green buildings on the hill.

01:00:59.368 --> 01:01:02.371
Think of all those roofs
you could climb over.

01:01:02.788 --> 01:01:05.583
- Who did you call me?
- Robie. John Robie.

01:01:05.666 --> 01:01:08.836
One of the world's cleverest
jewel thieves, known as the Cat.

01:01:08.919 --> 01:01:10.754
I read all about you in the Paris paper.

01:01:10.838 --> 01:01:13.215
You may have read
about somebody called the Cat, but...

01:01:13.299 --> 01:01:15.134
- I thought you said you were hungry.
- I am.

01:01:15.217 --> 01:01:17.636
Well, the picnic basket's in the trunk.

01:01:26.896 --> 01:01:28.856
I hope you try to bluff me, Mr. Robie,

01:01:28.939 --> 01:01:32.026
and then I can have the fun
of telling you how clever I was.

01:01:32.109 --> 01:01:34.236
Well, since I'm not Mr. Robie
but Mr. Burns,

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:36.989
there would be hardly any point
in trying to bluff you.

01:01:37.072 --> 01:01:38.824
Nevertheless, tell me
how clever you were.

01:01:38.908 --> 01:01:41.660
Well, the first thing
I noticed about you was...

01:01:41.744 --> 01:01:43.954
Don't sound so pleased with yourself.

01:01:44.038 --> 01:01:46.248
I've never caught a jewel thief before.
It's stimulating.

01:01:46.332 --> 01:01:50.294
- It's like... Well, it's like...
- Like sitting in a hot tub?

01:01:52.171 --> 01:01:54.215
Here, let me serve.

01:01:54.506 --> 01:01:57.092
The first time I saw you
was on the beach at Cannes.

01:01:57.176 --> 01:02:00.846
You swam ashore from a motorboat
driven by that little French girl.

01:02:00.930 --> 01:02:02.973
You got an opener? Thank you.

01:02:03.057 --> 01:02:06.602
- You want a leg or a breast?
- You make the choice.

01:02:07.311 --> 01:02:09.855
That was just two days
before you showed up at the hotel

01:02:09.939 --> 01:02:13.525
as Mr. Conrad Burns,
just over from America.

01:02:13.609 --> 01:02:15.152
Did you swim?

01:02:15.236 --> 01:02:16.528
Well, naturally.

01:02:16.612 --> 01:02:19.531
Oh, now, don't be disappointing
and sound like Mr. Burns.

01:02:19.615 --> 01:02:21.700
Well, I can only be myself,
Miss Stevens.

01:02:21.784 --> 01:02:24.495
Then be yourself, John.

01:02:24.578 --> 01:02:27.748
- I prefer Conrad.
- Oh, you can't be serious.

01:02:27.873 --> 01:02:31.085
And I think it's about time
you called me Francie.

01:02:31.794 --> 01:02:34.880
I have to look out for Mother.
They've tried to steal her jewelry before.

01:02:34.964 --> 01:02:38.050
So when I read about the...
You in the paper,

01:02:38.133 --> 01:02:42.471
just a small item, but I picked it up,
I was sure Mother would catch your eye.

01:02:42.554 --> 01:02:44.640
Well, she did, because I liked her.

01:02:44.932 --> 01:02:47.559
- And so far, Miss Stevens...
- Francie.

01:02:47.726 --> 01:02:50.771
So far you haven't said anything
that sounds even remotely clever.

01:02:50.854 --> 01:02:53.023
Well, stick around.
The next thing I noticed about you

01:02:53.107 --> 01:02:55.484
-was something remarkable.
- Yeah?

01:02:55.567 --> 01:02:59.196
All evening long, you only looked
at my mother, never at me.

01:02:59.363 --> 01:03:01.865
- I kissed you, didn't I?
- I kissed you.

01:03:02.658 --> 01:03:04.368
I certainly wasn't
looking at your mother then.

01:03:04.451 --> 01:03:05.703
You were thinking about her.

01:03:05.828 --> 01:03:08.122
Otherwise you never would have
let me say good night so easily.

01:03:08.205 --> 01:03:09.832
Well, I'm a gentleman.

01:03:10.040 --> 01:03:13.377
A rough lumberman
from the big Northwest?

01:03:13.794 --> 01:03:16.714
I must remember
to yell "timber" occasionally.

01:03:18.340 --> 01:03:21.051
Now, here comes
some of the clever part.

01:03:21.635 --> 01:03:24.138
You're just not convincing, John.

01:03:24.221 --> 01:03:27.391
You're like an American character
in an English movie.

01:03:27.516 --> 01:03:30.561
You just don't talk the way
an American tourist ought to talk.

01:03:30.686 --> 01:03:32.229
Don't you know
that all the guidebooks say,

01:03:32.313 --> 01:03:33.981
"Don't behave like a tourist"?

01:03:34.064 --> 01:03:37.568
Well, it's just that you never mention
business or baseball or television

01:03:37.693 --> 01:03:43.157
-or wage freezes or Senate probes.
- All the things I left America to forget.

01:03:43.741 --> 01:03:46.827
You're just not American enough
to carry it off.

01:03:47.578 --> 01:03:49.747
Tell me, how long has it been?

01:03:50.205 --> 01:03:53.500
- How long has what been?
- Since you were in America last?

01:03:53.584 --> 01:03:55.419
Oh, I don't know, about four or five days.

01:03:55.544 --> 01:03:58.339
- And Oregon?
- Two or three days before that.

01:03:58.422 --> 01:04:01.675
Name me three deciduous trees
indigenous to the Northwest.

01:04:02.092 --> 01:04:06.263
Now, listen, you're a very nice girl,
but you've got too much imagination.

01:04:06.388 --> 01:04:07.848
You go around
talking like that about me,

01:04:07.931 --> 01:04:10.184
and I'll wind up in a French jail
for something I didn't do.

01:04:10.267 --> 01:04:12.353
Are you going to rob Mother first
or somebody else?

01:04:12.436 --> 01:04:14.521
Well, under the circumstances,
somebody else.

01:04:14.605 --> 01:04:17.107
- That's nice. Mother likes you.
- Well...

01:04:17.232 --> 01:04:19.943
I think Lady Kenton
should be our next job.

01:04:20.069 --> 01:04:22.613
- Now, listen...
- Isn't she on your list? She ought to be.

01:04:22.738 --> 01:04:25.949
The Kenton jewels are famous.
I know every inch of her villa.

01:04:26.075 --> 01:04:29.536
- I can already hear your next line.
- The Cat has a new kitten.

01:04:29.620 --> 01:04:32.289
- When do we start?
- Don't talk like that.

01:04:32.956 --> 01:04:34.958
You're leaving fingerprints on my arm.

01:04:35.084 --> 01:04:38.921
- I am not John Robie, the Cat.
- Why are the police following you?

01:04:39.630 --> 01:04:41.048
Show me that real estate list.

01:04:41.131 --> 01:04:43.300
That villa we went to isn't for rent,
and you know it.

01:04:43.425 --> 01:04:47.054
The Sanfords have owned it for years,
and I'm going to a party there in a week.

01:04:47.137 --> 01:04:50.766
You have a very strong grip,
the kind a burglar needs.

01:05:03.320 --> 01:05:06.156
That's why you came up here, isn't it?

01:05:07.574 --> 01:05:11.245
We'll have cocktails at 8:00
and dinner at 8:30, all in my suite.

01:05:11.412 --> 01:05:14.748
- We'll talk about it there.
- I can't come.

01:05:14.832 --> 01:05:17.918
I'm going to the casino
and to watch a firework display.

01:05:18.001 --> 01:05:22.589
- You'll get a better view from my place.
- Already got another date.

01:05:23.090 --> 01:05:26.969
Everywhere you go, I'll have you paged
as John Robie, the Cat.

01:05:28.470 --> 01:05:30.806
8:00, and be on time.

01:05:31.265 --> 01:05:35.310
- I haven't got a decent watch.
- Steal one.

01:05:37.479 --> 01:05:40.941
Excuse me. I could not speak
with you today, <i>Monsieur</i> Robie.

01:05:41.024 --> 01:05:45.028
- I did not know your new name.
- I figured you'd have had a good reason.

01:05:45.154 --> 01:05:46.864
What were you doing
at the Sanford villa, anyway?

01:05:46.989 --> 01:05:49.450
I supply food and drinks
for the grand gala.

01:05:49.533 --> 01:05:51.702
I was inspecting the kitchen.

01:05:51.827 --> 01:05:54.788
<i>I do not ask you
what you were doing there.</i>

01:05:54.872 --> 01:05:56.707
<i>You prepare also, no?</i>

01:05:56.790 --> 01:06:00.461
<i>There will be many women, rich jewels.</i>

01:06:00.544 --> 01:06:02.129
Just the bait I need.

01:06:02.212 --> 01:06:04.506
Something the Cat can't resist, huh?

01:06:04.715 --> 01:06:08.177
- I suppose all your boys will be there?
<i>- Naturally.</i>

01:06:08.343 --> 01:06:10.888
That's nice. You know,
they threatened to put me away.

01:06:10.971 --> 01:06:15.058
Oh, Robie, they'd be much too busy
to do anything like that.

01:06:15.225 --> 01:06:18.145
- Well, you keep them busy.
<i>- Goodbye, Robie.</i>

01:06:18.479 --> 01:06:21.231
Oh, who was the pretty American girl?

01:06:21.982 --> 01:06:24.318
<i>You bring her
to my restaurant for dinner, yes?</i>

01:06:24.401 --> 01:06:27.488
Not tonight. She made
some small plans for the two of us.

01:06:27.571 --> 01:06:28.864
Well, soon.

01:06:29.072 --> 01:06:30.157
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:06:34.244 --> 01:06:35.329
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:06:35.579 --> 01:06:36.580
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:06:37.247 --> 01:06:38.415
Good night.

01:06:39.833 --> 01:06:44.505
If you really want to see fireworks,
it's better with the lights out.

01:06:44.588 --> 01:06:46.340
I have a feeling that tonight
you're going to see

01:06:46.423 --> 01:06:49.384
one of the Riviera's
most fascinating sights.

01:06:49.927 --> 01:06:53.388
- I was talking about the fireworks.
- I never doubted it.

01:06:53.847 --> 01:06:57.100
The way you looked at my necklace,
I didn't know.

01:06:57.851 --> 01:07:01.188
You've been dying to say something
about it all evening. Go ahead.

01:07:01.271 --> 01:07:05.567
- Why, have I been staring at it?
- No, you've been trying to avoid it.

01:07:05.943 --> 01:07:07.528
- May I have a brandy?
- Please.

01:07:07.611 --> 01:07:10.030
- Would you care for one?
- No, thank you.

01:07:12.741 --> 01:07:15.869
Some nights,
a person doesn't need to drink.

01:07:17.746 --> 01:07:20.374
Doesn't it make you nervous
to be in the same room

01:07:20.457 --> 01:07:22.876
with thousands of dollars' worth
of diamonds

01:07:22.960 --> 01:07:25.546
-and unable to touch them?
- No.

01:07:27.130 --> 01:07:30.634
Like an alcoholic outside of a bar
on election day.

01:07:31.426 --> 01:07:33.387
Wouldn't know the feeling.

01:07:33.470 --> 01:07:37.307
All right. You've studied the layout,
drawn your plans,

01:07:37.432 --> 01:07:39.893
worked out your timetable,
put on your dark clothes

01:07:39.977 --> 01:07:42.312
with your crepe-sole shoes
and your rope,

01:07:43.063 --> 01:07:44.731
maybe your face blackened.

01:07:44.815 --> 01:07:46.567
And you're over the roofs
in the darkness,

01:07:46.650 --> 01:07:49.486
down the side wall
to the right apartment,

01:07:49.611 --> 01:07:51.572
and the window's locked.

01:07:51.655 --> 01:07:55.909
All that elation turned into frustration.
What would you do?

01:07:56.618 --> 01:07:58.829
I'd go home, get a good night's sleep.

01:07:59.454 --> 01:08:01.832
Oh, what would you do?
The thrill is right there in front of you,

01:08:01.957 --> 01:08:03.834
but you can't quite get it.

01:08:03.917 --> 01:08:07.921
And the gems glistening
on the other side of the window.

01:08:08.005 --> 01:08:10.841
And someone asleep, breathing heavily.

01:08:11.425 --> 01:08:13.260
I'd go home, get a good night's sleep.

01:08:13.343 --> 01:08:15.929
Wouldn't you use a glass cutter,
a brick, your fist,

01:08:16.013 --> 01:08:17.931
anything to get what you wanted,

01:08:18.015 --> 01:08:20.851
knowing it was just there
waiting for you?

01:08:21.101 --> 01:08:24.771
- No, I wouldn't.
- Drinking dulls your senses.

01:08:24.855 --> 01:08:27.441
And, if I'm lucky, some of my hearing.

01:08:28.025 --> 01:08:32.529
Blue-white, with just
hair-like touches of platinum.

01:08:32.613 --> 01:08:34.531
You know, I have about
the same interest in jewelry

01:08:34.656 --> 01:08:36.950
that I have in politics,
horse racing, modern poetry

01:08:37.034 --> 01:08:38.452
or women who need weird excitement.

01:08:38.535 --> 01:08:39.786
None.

01:08:40.203 --> 01:08:41.955
Hold this necklace in your hand

01:08:42.039 --> 01:08:45.000
and tell me
you're not John Robie, the Cat.

01:08:46.001 --> 01:08:48.295
John, tell me something.

01:08:48.378 --> 01:08:51.548
You're going to rob that villa
we cased this afternoon, aren't you?

01:08:51.673 --> 01:08:55.636
Oh, I suppose "rob" is archaic.
You'd say "knock over"?

01:08:55.719 --> 01:08:58.472
- Oh, for...
- Don't worry. I'm very good at secrets.

01:08:58.555 --> 01:09:01.058
Tell me, have you ever been
on a psychiatrist's couch?

01:09:01.141 --> 01:09:02.643
Don't change the subject.

01:09:02.726 --> 01:09:04.978
I know the perfect time to do it.

01:09:05.062 --> 01:09:07.314
Next week, the Sanfords
are holding their annual gala.

01:09:07.397 --> 01:09:11.401
Everyone who counts will be there.
I'll get you an invitation.

01:09:11.485 --> 01:09:13.487
It's an 18th-century costume affair.

01:09:13.570 --> 01:09:15.572
There will be thousands
upon thousands of dollars' worth

01:09:15.697 --> 01:09:17.658
of the world's most elegant jewelry.

01:09:17.741 --> 01:09:20.077
Some of the guests will be staying
for the weekend.

01:09:20.202 --> 01:09:23.330
We'll get all the information and we'll
do it together. What do you say?

01:09:23.413 --> 01:09:26.583
My only comment
would be highly censurable.

01:09:31.171 --> 01:09:33.840
Give up, John. Admit who you are.

01:09:36.426 --> 01:09:40.013
Even in this light, I can tell
where your eyes are looking.

01:09:51.441 --> 01:09:54.444
Look, John. Hold them.

01:09:55.612 --> 01:10:00.409
Diamonds. The only thing
in the world you can't resist.

01:10:01.201 --> 01:10:04.621
Then tell me you don't know
what I'm talking about.

01:10:18.802 --> 01:10:21.722
Ever had a better offer
in your whole life?

01:10:21.805 --> 01:10:23.890
One with everything?

01:10:26.143 --> 01:10:28.311
I've never had a crazier one.

01:10:29.479 --> 01:10:32.065
Just as long as you're satisfied.

01:10:35.110 --> 01:10:38.613
You know as well as I do
this necklace is imitation.

01:10:39.948 --> 01:10:41.491
Well, I'm not.

01:11:18.320 --> 01:11:20.155
Give them back to me.

01:11:21.490 --> 01:11:23.158
What did you have in mind, Francie?

01:11:23.241 --> 01:11:25.994
Give them back to me. Mother's jewels.

01:11:27.037 --> 01:11:28.830
I don't have them.

01:11:33.376 --> 01:11:36.379
Now tell me...
Wait a minute. Whoa. No, no, no.

01:11:39.591 --> 01:11:42.260
- When did it happen?
- When I was asleep.

01:11:42.385 --> 01:11:43.428
Well, let's look.

01:11:43.553 --> 01:11:45.847
There's only one place to look,
and that's obviously here.

01:11:45.931 --> 01:11:47.599
Help yourself.

01:11:51.561 --> 01:11:54.606
Did you see Francie?
Did she tell you what happened?

01:11:54.731 --> 01:11:56.858
Yes. She's downstairs,
searching my room.

01:11:56.942 --> 01:11:59.653
Well, that doesn't make sense. She said
she knew where my jewelry was.

01:11:59.736 --> 01:12:01.321
Well, she was wrong.
May I look into your bedroom?

01:12:01.404 --> 01:12:03.156
If you think it'll do any good.
But don't you think

01:12:03.240 --> 01:12:04.866
we ought to call the police
and the hotel manager?

01:12:04.950 --> 01:12:06.701
You know, they always like
to get in on these things.

01:12:06.785 --> 01:12:08.829
Yes, but would you let me
look around first, Mrs. Stevens?

01:12:08.912 --> 01:12:12.040
I don't care. I'd be just as happy
if you didn't find anything.

01:12:12.374 --> 01:12:13.667
Why do you say that?

01:12:13.750 --> 01:12:16.169
Oh, I'm a little tired
of draping those things over me.

01:12:16.253 --> 01:12:17.504
It was exciting at first, but, you know,

01:12:17.587 --> 01:12:19.506
now I think it's more exciting
to have them stolen.

01:12:19.589 --> 01:12:21.049
Yes, and of course
you can't lose financially

01:12:21.132 --> 01:12:23.051
as long as Hughson is around
to make out the check.

01:12:23.134 --> 01:12:26.054
Well, I'd be crazy
to take this attitude if I did.

01:12:26.638 --> 01:12:29.266
Why on earth did Francie
suspect you, Mr. Burns,

01:12:29.724 --> 01:12:32.060
a simple woodcutter from Oregon?

01:12:32.143 --> 01:12:34.563
I'm anything but that, Mrs. Stevens.

01:12:34.729 --> 01:12:39.442
My real name is John Robie. I used
to be a jewel thief several years ago.

01:12:39.943 --> 01:12:42.571
Well, what a wonderful surprise.

01:12:43.238 --> 01:12:45.824
Somehow, I can't get worked up over it.

01:12:46.241 --> 01:12:49.911
- Where did you keep the stuff?
- In the case over there.

01:12:50.912 --> 01:12:54.165
- Watch out for fingerprints.
- There won't be any.

01:12:56.293 --> 01:12:59.296
- Now, did they get everything?
- Everything.

01:13:00.505 --> 01:13:02.966
Francie must have known about you
all along.

01:13:03.091 --> 01:13:06.636
She guessed today.
You must sleep soundly.

01:13:07.762 --> 01:13:09.264
I do.

01:13:12.309 --> 01:13:14.895
He came down through the air shaft.

01:13:14.978 --> 01:13:19.065
If you're not Mr. Burns from Oregon,
why do you call yourself that and not...

01:13:19.149 --> 01:13:22.152
- What was the other name?
- Robie. John Robie.

01:13:22.277 --> 01:13:23.945
- Oh, Mrs. Stevens...
- Oh, I know the whole story.

01:13:24.029 --> 01:13:25.864
You want to go straight,
but the gang won't let you.

01:13:25.989 --> 01:13:29.784
- Well, in this case, the gang is the law.
- Mother, don't talk to him.

01:13:30.660 --> 01:13:33.246
Don't touch anything.
You're not going to cover up any clues.

01:13:33.330 --> 01:13:36.041
But there aren't any clues to cover up.
He came down the air shaft,

01:13:36.166 --> 01:13:38.209
took the stones
and went away the same way.

01:13:38.335 --> 01:13:40.754
You know how he got in here
as well as I do.

01:13:40.837 --> 01:13:43.632
- Did you find anything in my room?
- I certainly did.

01:13:43.840 --> 01:13:47.010
- Nothing of your mother's.
- No, you gave those to your accomplice.

01:13:47.135 --> 01:13:50.472
But I did find that the clothes
of Mr. Burns, the American,

01:13:50.555 --> 01:13:54.184
all had French labels in them,
and I found this.

01:13:54.309 --> 01:13:57.145
A list of everyone on the Riviera
with jewelry worth stealing.

01:13:57.228 --> 01:13:59.564
Listen to what it says about us.

01:13:59.731 --> 01:14:03.568
What good is that going to do you?
You're already caught.

01:14:03.693 --> 01:14:06.029
I called the police from your room
and told them who you are

01:14:06.154 --> 01:14:07.656
and what you did tonight.

01:14:07.864 --> 01:14:12.160
Everything? Oh, the boys must have
enjoyed that down at headquarters.

01:14:13.411 --> 01:14:14.537
Mother, his name isn't Burns.

01:14:14.663 --> 01:14:17.165
He's a notorious jewel thief
called the Cat.

01:14:17.916 --> 01:14:22.462
And what's he doing here now, lamb,
if he already has got the junk?

01:14:22.545 --> 01:14:24.965
Returning to the scene of his crime.

01:14:25.048 --> 01:14:27.050
Since when is love a crime?

01:14:28.218 --> 01:14:30.971
His name is Robie,
and for my money he's a real man,

01:14:31.054 --> 01:14:33.139
not one of those milksops
you generally take up with.

01:14:33.223 --> 01:14:36.059
- Mother, after all...
- After all, my foot.

01:14:37.435 --> 01:14:40.188
Why do you think
we moved so many times?

01:14:40.397 --> 01:14:44.025
Your father was a swindler, dear,
but a lovable one.

01:14:44.109 --> 01:14:47.028
You ask me, this one's
a bigger operator on every level.

01:14:47.112 --> 01:14:48.989
- Thank you, <i>madame.</i>
- Mother, this is why

01:14:49.072 --> 01:14:52.075
I've had to spend half my life
traveling around the world after you,

01:14:52.200 --> 01:14:53.827
to keep men like this away from you.

01:14:53.910 --> 01:14:55.745
Well, after this,
let me run my own interference.

01:14:55.870 --> 01:14:58.415
Looks like the blockers
are having all the fun.

01:14:58.540 --> 01:15:01.209
Well, if she doesn't have
any common sense, I do.

01:15:01.292 --> 01:15:02.502
Oh, shut up.

01:15:02.585 --> 01:15:04.295
They were my baubles that were stolen.

01:15:04.421 --> 01:15:07.257
If I don't care, why should you?
They're insured.

01:15:07.716 --> 01:15:10.176
Now, the big question is,
where do we go from here?

01:15:10.260 --> 01:15:11.302
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

01:15:12.929 --> 01:15:14.389
To jail.

01:15:30.780 --> 01:15:32.282
Where is he?

01:15:32.615 --> 01:15:34.701
- Who?
- John Robie.

01:15:34.784 --> 01:15:36.494
Never heard of him.

01:15:40.790 --> 01:15:43.793
Mother, the book you're reading
is upside down.

01:15:55.430 --> 01:15:57.182
We may be in France,
but I'm still an American,

01:15:57.307 --> 01:15:59.684
and a man is innocent
until he's proved guilty.

01:15:59.809 --> 01:16:01.644
- Proved.
- That won't be hard.

01:16:01.770 --> 01:16:03.688
What's bothering you
is John Robie's the first man

01:16:03.813 --> 01:16:05.857
who wouldn't fall down
and roll over for you.

01:16:05.982 --> 01:16:08.777
Oh, Mother, he played us both for fools,
and you know it.

01:16:08.860 --> 01:16:10.737
I know you ought to be spanked
with a hairbrush

01:16:10.820 --> 01:16:12.614
and sent back to school, public school,

01:16:12.697 --> 01:16:14.908
where they could pound some sense
into you during recess.

01:16:14.991 --> 01:16:19.204
- He's a low, worthless thief.
- Just what did he steal from you?

01:16:19.788 --> 01:16:21.331
Oh, Mother!

01:16:21.456 --> 01:16:25.085
Sit down while I tell you something
about life and John Robie.

01:16:25.168 --> 01:16:27.087
Sit down before I knock you down.

01:16:27.170 --> 01:16:28.838
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:16:36.012 --> 01:16:37.013
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:16:49.818 --> 01:16:52.112
So for three days
you've been doing nothing but fishing.

01:16:52.195 --> 01:16:56.825
Keep it down.
Do you still believe I did the Stevens job?

01:16:57.033 --> 01:16:59.744
Well, I... Yes, until you sent for me.

01:16:59.869 --> 01:17:02.455
If you had done it, you'd hardly
risk my bringing the police with me.

01:17:02.539 --> 01:17:03.665
Thank you.

01:17:03.832 --> 01:17:05.917
As you've been safely in hiding
for several days,

01:17:06.042 --> 01:17:07.335
why did you come out in the open?

01:17:07.418 --> 01:17:10.630
- I need your help.
- Perhaps I need yours even more.

01:17:10.713 --> 01:17:12.841
You see, my superiors
at the London office...

01:17:12.924 --> 01:17:16.469
Well, this time I might solve some
of your problems, possibly all of them.

01:17:16.553 --> 01:17:18.012
That's too much to hope for.

01:17:18.096 --> 01:17:20.890
For three nights, I've been watching
one of the villas on your list.

01:17:21.015 --> 01:17:22.267
- Oh? Which one?
- The Silvas',

01:17:22.392 --> 01:17:23.643
that South American couple.

01:17:23.726 --> 01:17:26.271
Somebody else is watching it, too.
I've seen him in the dark,

01:17:26.396 --> 01:17:27.689
but I haven't managed
to get close enough

01:17:27.772 --> 01:17:29.315
to catch him and to find out who it is.

01:17:29.399 --> 01:17:31.568
- Has he seen you?
- Probably.

01:17:31.693 --> 01:17:33.778
I want to set a foolproof trap
for him tonight.

01:17:33.903 --> 01:17:36.281
To do that, I'm going to need
the assistance of the police.

01:17:36.406 --> 01:17:38.408
Naturally, I can't approach them.

01:17:38.533 --> 01:17:40.577
Well, how do you know
he'll be there tonight?

01:17:40.702 --> 01:17:43.705
Somebody gave this to Germaine,
my housekeeper.

01:17:45.957 --> 01:17:47.792
But it's in French. What does it say?

01:17:47.917 --> 01:17:50.837
Roughly, it says, "Robie, stay away
from the Silvas' villa tonight.

01:17:50.920 --> 01:17:53.047
"It's my night to yowl, not yours."

01:17:53.131 --> 01:17:56.259
- Who gave it to Germaine?
- It was left in her shopping basket.

01:17:57.427 --> 01:18:00.096
Look, Hughson,
get back to town and convince Lepic

01:18:00.221 --> 01:18:03.433
to have the police at the villa
soon after midnight.

01:18:03.725 --> 01:18:05.810
- You're actually going there?
- Of course.

01:18:05.935 --> 01:18:08.479
But, Robie, that note
is obviously bait for a trap.

01:18:08.605 --> 01:18:10.648
Someone wants you
to go to the Silvas' tonight.

01:18:10.773 --> 01:18:13.318
- I know it.
- Possibly to kill you.

01:18:14.444 --> 01:18:17.280
- Will you talk to Lepic?
- All right,

01:18:17.614 --> 01:18:21.201
but, look, if this Cat doesn't show up,
the police might get you,

01:18:21.284 --> 01:18:23.953
and the whole thing
will turn out rather badly.

01:18:24.454 --> 01:18:27.332
No, maybe I'd better
go along tonight as your alibi.

01:18:27.457 --> 01:18:29.918
Hughson, I know you get
your insurance rates at a discount,

01:18:30.001 --> 01:18:31.836
but why be foolhardy?

01:19:44.784 --> 01:19:45.868
(MAN SHOUTING IN FRENCH)

01:19:55.211 --> 01:19:56.254
(MEN GRUNTING)

01:20:25.408 --> 01:20:28.411
(OFFICER SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:20:35.084 --> 01:20:37.337
Almost everybody in Philadelphia
reads <i>The Bulletin.</i>

01:20:37.420 --> 01:20:39.255
Just a minute, Mother.

01:20:47.263 --> 01:20:51.309
- What is it? What's all the excitement?
- The cat burglar's dead.

01:20:51.601 --> 01:20:55.146
- John Robie?
- No, a man named Foussard,

01:20:55.271 --> 01:20:57.440
a wine steward in a restaurant.

01:20:57.940 --> 01:21:02.153
Honey, you better start practicing
your apologies in two languages.

01:21:05.323 --> 01:21:07.825
You are positive Foussard was the Cat?

01:21:07.950 --> 01:21:10.995
We have no reason to change
the story I gave to the newspaper.

01:21:11.120 --> 01:21:13.247
Well, that's hardly
a direct answer, Commissioner.

01:21:13.331 --> 01:21:16.584
I cannot give you another.
Now, if you'll excuse me...

01:21:16.667 --> 01:21:20.838
One more point, <i>Monsieur</i> Lepic.
This is a check for $280,000.

01:21:21.172 --> 01:21:23.466
That's nearly 92 million francs.

01:21:23.800 --> 01:21:26.344
Now, since you caught
and unfortunately killed the Cat...

01:21:26.469 --> 01:21:29.138
In our opinion, he killed himself
attempting to escape justice.

01:21:29.222 --> 01:21:31.391
Yeah, well, either way,
I've been instructed by my company

01:21:31.474 --> 01:21:33.434
to pay off the Stevens' claim.

01:21:33.518 --> 01:21:37.480
Now, I'm disinclined to do this
if recovery of the jewelry is imminent.

01:21:37.647 --> 01:21:38.648
Is it?

01:21:38.731 --> 01:21:42.485
- It will take time.
- I'd say several centuries.

01:21:43.820 --> 01:21:47.907
I just came in to congratulate you
on your capture, <i>Commissaire.</i>

01:21:50.076 --> 01:21:51.911
All's well that ends well, huh?

01:21:53.162 --> 01:21:56.749
Yes, newspapers have their headlines,
all the rich tourists can relax,

01:21:56.833 --> 01:21:58.459
and you, Lepic, got your publicity

01:21:58.543 --> 01:22:00.795
and possibly a commendation
from the Paris office.

01:22:00.878 --> 01:22:02.839
Almost everyone
got some good out of it.

01:22:02.922 --> 01:22:05.049
Oh, except, of course,
Hughson's company.

01:22:05.174 --> 01:22:07.176
But then, they can well afford it,
can't they, Hughson?

01:22:07.260 --> 01:22:09.512
Well, it has cut into their assets.

01:22:10.346 --> 01:22:13.015
Poor Foussard.
Never guessed it was him.

01:22:13.099 --> 01:22:17.395
Ordinary wine waiter.
Family man. Wooden leg.

01:22:20.773 --> 01:22:24.861
Oh, didn't you know?
Why, certainly. He lost it during the war.

01:22:25.236 --> 01:22:26.821
You know, that's remarkable, isn't it?

01:22:26.904 --> 01:22:29.490
A man with a wooden leg
teaching himself to climb up walls

01:22:29.574 --> 01:22:32.869
and to run over the roof
with the agility of a four-footed cat.

01:22:33.035 --> 01:22:36.456
- Is that true?
- I believe he had a bad leg.

01:22:36.581 --> 01:22:40.084
Yes, you showed such good taste
in keeping it out of the newspapers.

01:22:40.877 --> 01:22:43.588
Well, I think it only fitting
that I drop into Foussard's funeral,

01:22:43.713 --> 01:22:45.006
pay my last respects.

01:22:45.089 --> 01:22:47.550
Oh, and at the same time
get a look at the real Cat,

01:22:47.633 --> 01:22:50.052
who will certainly be there purring.

01:22:50.386 --> 01:22:54.056
- You know who the real Cat is?
- In a phrase, I do.

01:22:54.140 --> 01:22:57.435
- Well, tell the Commissioner who it is.
- He wouldn't believe me.

01:22:57.560 --> 01:23:00.897
- Well, then try me.
- You'd find it hard to believe.

01:23:01.314 --> 01:23:04.609
But when I catch the Cat on a rooftop
with a handful of stolen diamonds...

01:23:04.734 --> 01:23:08.237
<i>Monsieur</i> Robie, it's because
I gave this story to the newspapers

01:23:08.321 --> 01:23:09.572
you're at liberty.

01:23:09.655 --> 01:23:13.910
But the day I catch you on a roof,
I'll call the reporters again.

01:23:13.993 --> 01:23:15.953
Lepic, that's all I wanted to know.

01:23:16.078 --> 01:23:17.455
Good day.

01:23:19.499 --> 01:23:21.959
(PRIEST RECITING PRAYER IN LATIN)

01:24:07.630 --> 01:24:10.633
A most unhappy affair, eh, Robie?

01:24:11.300 --> 01:24:13.970
Unhappy because it isn't me
down there?

01:24:16.305 --> 01:24:17.723
Poor Danielle.

01:24:18.516 --> 01:24:21.018
I have a great compassion for her.

01:24:22.144 --> 01:24:23.980
I'll look out for her.

01:24:25.314 --> 01:24:27.984
What do you suppose happened
to all the stuff he stole?

01:24:28.150 --> 01:24:29.986
That's a mystery.

01:24:30.319 --> 01:24:32.863
The police have looked in every place.

01:24:33.406 --> 01:24:35.324
Someday it'll turn up.

01:24:35.491 --> 01:24:39.161
- The boys owe you many thanks.
- For what?

01:24:39.495 --> 01:24:43.165
You know, for risking the prison
to capture the Cat.

01:24:43.833 --> 01:24:45.668
Oh, that.

01:24:46.210 --> 01:24:50.006
But you, you have no reason
to complain, eh?

01:24:51.007 --> 01:24:53.384
Could you be a little more specific?

01:24:53.884 --> 01:24:56.679
The American girl... What's her name?

01:24:56.846 --> 01:25:00.016
Oh, Francie Stevens. That the one?

01:25:00.933 --> 01:25:02.268
What luck.

01:25:02.893 --> 01:25:09.191
A beautiful woman with love for you,
rich beyond your dreams.

01:25:09.734 --> 01:25:11.527
I dream pretty rich.

01:25:11.694 --> 01:25:15.531
- When are you going to America?
- I didn't know I was.

01:25:17.116 --> 01:25:21.287
You will make a great mistake
if you don't marry her

01:25:21.454 --> 01:25:24.040
and return to your native country.

01:25:24.790 --> 01:25:29.086
Tell you what. Let's talk about it
at the Sanford gala over the weekend,

01:25:29.211 --> 01:25:31.505
you know, between your catering duties.

01:25:31.589 --> 01:25:34.759
- You are not invited.
- I will be.

01:25:36.719 --> 01:25:38.804
What costume will you wear?

01:25:39.388 --> 01:25:42.058
I'll figure out something to surprise you.

01:25:43.559 --> 01:25:45.019
Good luck.

01:25:45.102 --> 01:25:49.148
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:25:58.240 --> 01:25:59.408
Killer!

01:26:00.159 --> 01:26:01.744
It's because of you he's dead.

01:26:01.827 --> 01:26:04.163
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:26:04.246 --> 01:26:05.289
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:26:07.917 --> 01:26:09.168
Get out!

01:26:09.919 --> 01:26:12.004
Get out of here, killer.

01:26:12.088 --> 01:26:13.172
(DANIELLE SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:26:13.255 --> 01:26:14.590
Murderer!

01:26:41.701 --> 01:26:42.952
FRANCIE: John?

01:26:50.126 --> 01:26:51.293
John.

01:27:10.980 --> 01:27:13.607
Are you going to make it hard
for me to apologize?

01:27:13.691 --> 01:27:16.902
- Not at all. I'm sure you're sorry.
- You know I am.

01:27:17.653 --> 01:27:20.614
Till Mother told me, I had no idea
of the things you were up against.

01:27:20.698 --> 01:27:22.074
Yeah, well...

01:27:22.950 --> 01:27:24.952
What are your plans now?

01:27:25.369 --> 01:27:28.164
- Now what?
- That the cat burglar's dead.

01:27:28.247 --> 01:27:30.583
- Foussard isn't the Cat.
- But the newspapers...

01:27:30.666 --> 01:27:32.251
The man had a wooden leg.

01:27:32.334 --> 01:27:34.795
Wasn't he caught at a villa
trying to rob it?

01:27:34.879 --> 01:27:37.965
He wasn't trying to rob it.
He was trying to kill me.

01:27:38.048 --> 01:27:39.133
Why?

01:27:39.216 --> 01:27:41.010
Because I was getting
too close to the Cat.

01:27:41.093 --> 01:27:45.431
- Well, then who killed him?
- If I ever find that out, I'll let you know.

01:27:45.514 --> 01:27:48.768
- Goodbye, Francie.
- John, why bother?

01:27:50.144 --> 01:27:52.813
You know, it's sort of a hobby of mine,
the truth.

01:27:53.189 --> 01:27:55.691
- Let me do something to help you.
- Oh, no, thanks.

01:27:55.775 --> 01:27:57.193
Now, you've just made your apologies.

01:27:57.276 --> 01:28:00.446
Let's go back to our mutual disregard
of each other.

01:28:01.071 --> 01:28:04.700
Mr. Robie,
I was wrong about you, I think.

01:28:05.201 --> 01:28:07.745
You might possibly be wrong about me.

01:28:08.704 --> 01:28:10.790
Well, now, that's another thing
that I may never know.

01:28:10.873 --> 01:28:11.957
Now if you'll pardon me,

01:28:12.041 --> 01:28:14.210
- I have a bus to catch.
- I won't pardon you.

01:28:16.462 --> 01:28:18.380
I'm in love with you.

01:28:22.843 --> 01:28:27.181
- Well, that's a ridiculous thing to say.
- Is it?

01:28:28.098 --> 01:28:31.560
You know, to you,
words are just playthings.

01:28:31.644 --> 01:28:33.229
Were playthings.

01:28:35.940 --> 01:28:38.818
I'll make you a sporting, exciting offer.

01:28:40.611 --> 01:28:42.738
I don't know if I'm up to it now.

01:28:43.197 --> 01:28:46.575
Get me an invitation to the Sanford gala.
I'll take you there.

01:28:46.659 --> 01:28:49.662
It's costume, you know.
You can't go without a costume.

01:28:49.745 --> 01:28:51.747
- What are you wearing?
- Louis XV.

01:28:51.831 --> 01:28:54.792
Mother and I got them in Paris.

01:28:56.669 --> 01:28:58.921
I'll phone you in a day or two.

01:28:59.922 --> 01:29:03.008
Oh, you probably wonder
why I want to go, huh?

01:29:03.259 --> 01:29:04.510
I have an idea.

01:29:04.593 --> 01:29:08.556
I thought you might like to see
a real, live burglar in action.

01:29:09.765 --> 01:29:11.600
Will it be dangerous?

01:29:13.143 --> 01:29:14.770
Not for tourists.

01:32:08.819 --> 01:32:10.571
ROBIE: Well, we're in.

01:32:10.654 --> 01:32:13.574
Any man without a lady on his arm
can only be a policeman.

01:32:14.199 --> 01:32:16.410
My nerves could stand a drink.

01:32:16.702 --> 01:32:18.871
Your nerves and your mother.

01:32:40.142 --> 01:32:45.564
(ALL SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:32:57.618 --> 01:32:58.660
There they are.

01:32:58.744 --> 01:33:01.371
Police Commissioner Lepic
and one of his men.

01:33:01.455 --> 01:33:03.665
Wigs, pantaloons and flat feet.

01:33:04.208 --> 01:33:05.542
Come on.

01:33:08.796 --> 01:33:09.838
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:33:10.422 --> 01:33:11.507
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:33:11.590 --> 01:33:13.342
Oh, my heart pills.

01:33:13.425 --> 01:33:14.927
I can't drink champagne without my pills.

01:33:15.010 --> 01:33:16.095
It gives me heartburn.

01:33:16.178 --> 01:33:18.055
Where do you think
you left them, Mother?

01:33:18.138 --> 01:33:20.766
Upstairs in our room.
They're in my purse.

01:33:20.849 --> 01:33:23.227
Would you be a sweetie, John,
and go and get them for me?

01:33:23.310 --> 01:33:25.395
ROBIE: Of course. I'd be delighted.

01:33:26.188 --> 01:33:28.774
- Mother.
- Well, all I said was...

01:33:28.857 --> 01:33:30.818
Never mind what you said.

01:33:49.628 --> 01:33:52.756
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:34:17.656 --> 01:34:20.117
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:34:34.882 --> 01:34:36.633
Thank you, dear.

01:34:40.512 --> 01:34:42.139
Shall we dance?

01:35:01.950 --> 01:35:03.035
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:37:58.627 --> 01:38:00.587
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:38:19.815 --> 01:38:23.902
Well, I hope the London office
appreciate what I've done for them.

01:38:24.694 --> 01:38:26.571
My feet are killing me.

01:38:27.322 --> 01:38:29.074
Mother was quite a little actress tonight.

01:38:29.157 --> 01:38:32.702
Yes, I thought she played her part
rather well. "Heart pills."

01:38:34.830 --> 01:38:38.834
Frankly, I didn't believe this scheme
of yours would work, Francie, but it has.

01:39:27.382 --> 01:39:29.009
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:39:57.078 --> 01:39:58.121
(CLATTERING)

01:41:08.650 --> 01:41:09.693
(CLATTERING)

01:41:28.503 --> 01:41:30.463
I figured it was you
the night your father died.

01:41:30.547 --> 01:41:31.590
He couldn't climb anything,

01:41:31.673 --> 01:41:33.008
and you always did his legwork for him,

01:41:33.091 --> 01:41:34.718
even during the war
when you were a kid.

01:41:34.801 --> 01:41:36.761
OFFICER: Come down, Robie!

01:41:50.108 --> 01:41:52.986
Come down, Robie,
or we shall be forced to shoot.

01:41:53.153 --> 01:41:55.363
(SPEAKING FRENCH)

01:42:25.560 --> 01:42:27.812
(GUN FIRING)

01:42:27.896 --> 01:42:29.564
Put that gun down. He's not the Cat.

01:42:29.648 --> 01:42:32.317
- Then what does he do on that roof?
- Your job. That's what he's doing.

01:42:32.400 --> 01:42:35.153
<i>- Madame,</i> I can only believe what I see.
- You shoot him and I'll...

01:42:35.236 --> 01:42:37.906
John Robie is just where
I knew he'd be someday.

01:42:51.920 --> 01:42:53.213
(GUN FIRING)

01:42:53.546 --> 01:42:55.256
HUGHSON: He's not alone up there.

01:43:37.090 --> 01:43:38.299
Oh, pull me up!

01:43:40.552 --> 01:43:43.138
Don't shout. It makes me nervous.
I might drop you.

01:43:43.221 --> 01:43:44.723
- Then drop me.
- Whatever you say.

01:43:44.806 --> 01:43:46.141
No!

01:43:46.266 --> 01:43:47.892
All right, now,
you've got a full house down there.

01:43:47.976 --> 01:43:50.145
- Begin the performance.
- What performance?

01:43:50.270 --> 01:43:51.730
You know,
the one in which you tell them

01:43:51.813 --> 01:43:53.314
what is what and who is really who.

01:43:53.398 --> 01:43:55.567
But, please, John, I might slip.

01:43:55.650 --> 01:43:58.737
I figure I can hold you
for about 30 seconds, no more.

01:43:58.820 --> 01:44:00.655
I did it for my father.

01:44:00.739 --> 01:44:02.407
That's fine, but I already know it.

01:44:02.490 --> 01:44:04.409
We're telling them
down there, remember?

01:44:04.492 --> 01:44:06.661
Oh, I'll kill you when I get up there.

01:44:06.786 --> 01:44:08.997
If you get up here. Tell them.

01:44:09.330 --> 01:44:12.917
I was working for my father!
Now, please...

01:44:13.001 --> 01:44:15.837
Your father is conveniently dead.
Who else?

01:44:15.920 --> 01:44:16.921
That's all.

01:44:17.005 --> 01:44:19.257
You know, I'm out of training.
My fingers are beginning to open.

01:44:19.340 --> 01:44:21.426
Tell them who was behind it,
who engineered the whole thing,

01:44:21.509 --> 01:44:23.678
who knew as much about me
as I knew myself.

01:44:24.179 --> 01:44:25.638
Go on! Go on!

01:44:26.931 --> 01:44:29.768
Bertani. Bertani's was behind it.

01:44:29.851 --> 01:44:31.102
We're telling them
down there, remember?

01:44:31.186 --> 01:44:33.188
Bertani's was behind it!

01:44:33.313 --> 01:44:35.857
- That's right.
- Oh, no! Please, please.

01:45:05.845 --> 01:45:07.972
- Who brought you up here?
- The police.

01:45:08.056 --> 01:45:10.809
And we would have caught you, too,
if my dress hadn't gotten caught

01:45:10.892 --> 01:45:13.144
all over the steering wheel and gearshift.

01:45:13.228 --> 01:45:14.979
It was only 20 minutes ago
I said goodbye.

01:45:15.063 --> 01:45:16.481
As quickly as you could.

01:45:16.564 --> 01:45:18.149
- Didn't I thank you?
- Politely.

01:45:18.233 --> 01:45:19.317
Well, then...

01:45:19.400 --> 01:45:21.986
Oh, John, you left in such a hurry,
you almost ran.

01:45:22.070 --> 01:45:24.030
I had work to do up here.

01:45:24.155 --> 01:45:28.201
Were you afraid to admit that you just
can't do everything by yourself,

01:45:28.576 --> 01:45:31.412
and that you needed the help
of a good woman,

01:45:31.871 --> 01:45:34.833
and you just aren't the lone wolf
you think you are?

01:45:34.916 --> 01:45:37.585
All right.
Without you, I couldn't have done it.

01:45:37.752 --> 01:45:39.879
I needed the help of a woman.

01:45:40.547 --> 01:45:44.008
I guess I'm not the lone wolf
I thought I was, Francie.

01:45:45.927 --> 01:45:48.888
Well, I just wanted to hear you say that.

01:45:50.098 --> 01:45:51.432
Thank you.

01:45:53.726 --> 01:45:55.770
- Goodbye.
- Goodbye.

01:46:09.617 --> 01:46:12.036
So this is where you live.

01:46:12.120 --> 01:46:14.789
Oh, Mother will love it up here.
